---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { allPosts, builtAt, url } from '../lib/data.js';
const matched = allPosts.filter((p) => Object.keys(p.matches).length).length;
---
<Base title="About">
  <article class="about">
    <h1>About this ordo</h1>
    <p>
      This is a proof of concept that indexes the <em>What Does The Prayer Really Say?</em> commentary from
      <a href="https://wdtprs.com">wdtprs.com</a> by liturgical day. Every post stays on Fr. Z’s site; this page shows only
      the prayer, a short excerpt, and a link to the full post.
    </p>

    <h2>How posts are placed on the calendar</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Posts are read from wdtprs.com’s public feed: the WDTPRS and PRAYERCAzT categories, plus prayer posts filed elsewhere.</li>
      <li>
        Each title names a liturgical day (“17th Sunday after Pentecost”, “28th Ordinary Sunday”, “Tuesday in Holy Week”). That
        name is matched against every day in the 1962 calendar (the 2020 USA calendar, generated with
        <a href="https://github.com/DivinumOfficium/divinum-officium">Divinum Officium</a>) and the
        current General Roman Calendar for the USA (computed with <a href="https://github.com/romcal/romcal">romcal</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Markers like “Novus Ordo”, “2002MR”, “Vetus Ordo”, “EF”, or names that exist in only one calendar (“after Pentecost”,
        “Ordinary Time”) decide which calendar a post belongs to.
      </li>
      <li>
        On weekdays without their own proper, the Mass repeats the preceding Sunday’s collect, so those days show that Sunday’s
        commentary.
      </li>
      <li>
        Where a 2020 addition for the USA replaces the plain 1962 celebration (St. Thomas Aquinas on a Lent weekday,
        for example), commentary on the replaced day and on the day’s commemorations is still shown.
      </li>
      <li>Posts on the same Latin prayer are linked across both calendars, since many collects moved between the Missals.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted">{allPosts.length} posts indexed, {matched} placed on the calendar. Last refreshed {builtAt?.slice(0, 10)}.</p>
    <p><a href={url('search/')}>Search the commentary</a> or <a href={url('today/')}>open today’s ordo</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about { max-width: 760px; }
  li { margin-bottom: .5em; }
</style>
